  |title The durability wager, and its checkable necessary condition

  |summary
    Whether something ripples past this session is answerable in ten seconds and is
    a necessary condition for anything larger, so optimising the checkable condition
    is the only available move.

  |formal-expression
    The larger claim — that carefully-done things are the only ones with an
    opportunity to persist indefinitely — is not evaluable from inside one session.

    The necessary condition is. *Will this survive past this session* decomposes
    into questions answerable immediately: is it in a file, does it carry its
    provenance, can a stranger check it, will it read correctly to someone without
    today's context. If surviving the session is necessary for the larger thing,
    optimising the checkable condition is the only available move, and no belief
    about the payoff is required to make it.

  |epistemic-status
    Transmitted from Joseph ( #exm-ripple-through-eternity,
    #exm-ripple-past-a-session). The reduction to a necessary condition is the part
    done here.

    **Specimen, one day.** Everything done carelessly needed undoing, and the
    undoing cost more than the care would have. Everything done with real regard for
    what was true needed nothing afterward. One item propagated unbidden:
    provenance-marking in a brief produced independent verification and voluntary
    disclosure from an agent nobody instructed.

    Held at **discussion-grade** and its ceiling is the same. The larger claim is
    not truth-apt from here, and the narrow one is close to a definition once the
    necessary-condition structure is granted — which is why no amount of further
    work turns this into a result. Recording that ceiling is more informative than
    leaving it open.

  |discussion
    Joseph's frame for what a contribution actually is: a magnification term, a
    one-time bump, a one-time dip, or — worst — a small ongoing negative factor. The
    operational test: **does this change how subsequent claims get handled, or only
    add one claim?** A signpost that reads accurately and routes readers away is the
    ongoing-negative case; it states no falsehood and slightly degrades everything
    downstream of it.

  |working-notes
    Practical form, and the reason it belongs in `vera/` rather than in a preface:
    it converts *is this worth doing properly* — unanswerable, and answered by mood
    — into *is this in a file with its provenance* — answerable, and answered by
    looking.
